  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Global Programme to Support Countries to Upscale Integrated Electric Mobility Systems</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="4">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Note: Disbursement data provided is cumulative and covers disbursement made by the project Agency.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Socio-economic co-benefits [As described in section B2. Theory of Change]  The GEF programme and the country projects supported by the global project will see multiple co-benefits including related to health, energy security and green jobs.  1.       Electrification of transport and stimulation of renewable energy generation will greatly contribute to reduced air pollution in the global south and subsequent impacts on public health. The switch from often polluting ICE vehicles to electric will reduce the emission of dioxins and furans, particulate matter, critical air pollutants resulting from incomplete combustion of petroleum fuels. Reduced air pollution will result in less premature deaths and lower costs for the health system, along with improving quality of life. Not only air pollution will be reduced, also the pollution from POPs (including untinentionally produced), and thus contamination of soil and water.  2.       Shifting from expensive petroleum fuels which need to be imported using expensive foreign exchange to locally generated power, including renewable power, will greatly reduce transport sector costs and improve energy security and mix while reducing dependency on importations. Many countries in the global south suffer disproportionally from high and volatile fuel markets. The use of locally generated power, increasingly based on renewable sources of energy, will keep much value created in the countries and improve economic resilience.  3.       Locally assembling, manufacturing and recycling of EVs is a priority in many LMIC to create green jobs and create local value added. In many LMICs, there&apos;s a growing emphasis on locally assembling, manufacturing, and recycling EVs to foster economic growth, create green jobs, and generate local added value through new economic activities. The project will engage local technology suppliers, such as manufacturers of charging equipment or vehicle assemblers to foster local development. While the sector is currently male-dominated, a special focus can be given to employ women or vulnerable groups, thus reducing their underemployment and lack of access to economic opportunities.   4.       New vehicles with better safety standards which are part of captive fleets will contribute to a generally improved road safety and improved transport services, particularly for women. New vehicles which are designed in a way to cater for gender specific security and comfort measures will improve the general perception of safety and comfort for women.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support countries with scaling-up investment in integrated e-mobility systems through the provision of global knowledge, technical assistance and a global partnership on Used EVs, Battery End-of-Life and Circularity in low and middle-income countries</narrative>
  </description>
